{
  "id": "task_001",
  "title": "Expression Evaluator (with precedence and unary minus)",
  "language": "python",
  "difficulty": "hard",
  "source": "LeetCode #772 variant (Basic Calculator III + unary)",
  "description": "Write a Python function `calculate(expression: str) -> int` that evaluates an arithmetic expression and returns its integer value.\n\nThe expression contains non-negative integers, the binary operators `+`, `-`, `*`, `/`, parentheses `(` and `)`, and spaces. A `-` may also be a UNARY minus (e.g. `-3`, `2*-3`, `-(1+2)`). Operator precedence is standard: `*` and `/` bind tighter than `+` and `-`; parentheses override precedence. Integer division must TRUNCATE TOWARD ZERO (so `-7/2 == -3` and `6/-4 == -1`). The expression is always valid.\n\nFunction signature:\ndef calculate(expression: str) -> int:",
  "function_signature": "def calculate(expression: str) -> int:",
  "constraints": [
    "1 <= len(expression) <= 10^4",
    "Integer division truncates toward zero",
    "Result fits in a 64-bit signed integer"
  ],
